[38;5;252m[1m### Two checks worth calling out[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The NUL-prefix comparison every base64 test naturally reaches for is not sufficient here, because the C never writes a terminator; it relies on [38;5;10mcalloc[0m zeroing the tail. I added whole-buffer parity over the full [38;5;10mcap[0m bytes for lengths 1–300 in both length modes, so a translation that wrote its own NUL or used [38;5;10mmalloc[0m + partial fill would be caught. Separately, [38;5;10msize == 0[0m means "call [38;5;10mstrlen[0m", and that [38;5;10msize_t[0m result is truncated to [38;5;10mint[0m — the most translation-sensitive line in the library. A 2 GiB string makes [38;5;10mstrlen[0m return [38;5;10m2^31[0m, truncating to [38;5;10mINT_MIN[0m, wrapping [38;5;10msize*4[0m to [38;5;10m0[0m, so a 2 GiB input encodes to the [1mempty string[22m. Both implementations do exactly that.[0m[0m
